  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\n\nSunday into Monday will see the remnants of the H5 longwave trof \neject from the Four Corners area northeast toward the Central \nPlains. Increased mid-level forcing for ascent combined with \nsoutherly to southwesterly flow bringing in a stream of moisture\nshould lead to more showers and storms. Northeast Nebraska \nlooks to hit the jackpot, with 60 to 80% PoPs progged for most \nof Sunday and Monday. 13z NBM probabilities suggest areas along \nand west of a line from Columbus to Norfolk with a 50 to 70% \nchance at seeing an inch or more of rain by Monday evening. \nProbabilities for even a quarter of an inch taper off sharply \nwith eastward extent, as Omaha and Lincoln see anywhere from a \n20 to 30% chance for a quarter inch of rain. \n\nThe H5 disturbance and lingering precip will depart the area early \nTuesday. Model spread starts to appear from Wednesday onward, with \nboth timing and placement of mid level features varying amongst \nguidance. Most key in on another H5 wave ejecting from somewhere in \nthe Intermountain West area toward the Dakotas, which could provide \nthe impetus needed for more rain chances Wednesday and Thursday.\nNBM suggests chances remain low though at 20-30%. Temperatures \nduring the long term will range from the 50s to 60s for most \nlocations.",
